WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:09.040
In 452 AD, Attila the Hun led a huge army&nbsp;
of allied Germanic and Asiatic warriors into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:17.680
northern Italy. Legend has it that Attila, who the&nbsp;
Romans termed Flagellum Dei, ‘the scourge of god’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:25.280
was only stopped when Pope Leo I met the warlord&nbsp;
and convinced him to leave Italy. Unlike Alaric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:36.320
the Goth in 410 AD or the Vandal King Gaiseric in&nbsp;
455 AD, Attila never sacked the city of Rome. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:42.960
why is he remembered today as the most fearsome of&nbsp;
the barbarian warlords who destroyed the Western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:51.840
Roman Empire? This is the story of Attila&nbsp;
the Hun, ‘the scourge of god’.  
&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:12.160
The man known to history as Attila the Hun&nbsp;
was born early in the fifth century AD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:19.200
perhaps around the year 406 AD. There is no&nbsp;
tangible evidence about his earliest years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.960
to establish when he was born and historians&nbsp;
have simply extrapolated his date of birth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.160
from the fact that, he was seemingly in his&nbsp;
thirties when he first came to prominence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:37.840
in the mid-430s AD. Similarly, his place of&nbsp;
birth is unclear, though given what we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.640
about the westward movement of the Huns in&nbsp;
the first years of the fifth century, he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:49.600
more than likely born somewhere around western&nbsp;
Ukraine, Moldova or northern Romania. 
&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:57.440
There is an equal lack of certainty when it&nbsp;
comes to Attila’s family background. His father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:04.720
was a prominent Hun warlord who was variously&nbsp;
referred to as Mundzuk, Mundzucus and Mundiucus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:12.400
by Greek and Roman scholars of the fifth and sixth&nbsp;
centuries AD. There is no clear indication of who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:19.280
Attila’s mother was. Prominent warlords amongst&nbsp;
the Huns practiced polygamy and Attila could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:25.840
been born to a concubine or one of several wives&nbsp;
of Mundzuk. We do know that he had a brother named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:32.400
Bleda, who became an important figure in events&nbsp;
later in Attila’s life. His father was a brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:38.480
of the Hun leaders, Rugila and Octar, and Attila&nbsp;
was consequently born into the ruling kinship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:45.040
group amongst the Huns at the start of the fifth&nbsp;
century AD. Despite his origin amongst the Huns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:51.520
a people who had migrated across Eurasia from the&nbsp;
Asian Steppe in the centuries prior to his birth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:58.720
there is a strong argument that Attila’s name&nbsp;
is Germanic. Atta roughly means ‘father’ in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:06.720
East Germanic dialects like Gothic and the ila&nbsp;
suffix means ‘little’. Thus, Attila would mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:13.680
‘little father’ if the name was Germanic. Cultural&nbsp;
osmosis of the kind that would lead a warlord like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:19.840
Mundzuk amongst the Asiatic Huns to call his&nbsp;
son by a Germanic name, was commonplace in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:26.160
the late Roman imperial period. An alternative&nbsp;
theory holds that the name comes from a Turkic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:36.320
dialect and means ‘great ocean’ or ‘old sea’. 
Reconstructing the life of Attila the Hun poses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:43.360
some difficulties. Firstly, there is the question&nbsp;
of bias in the sources. The Huns were not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.160
literate people and they did not have chroniclers&nbsp;
who could write accounts of their actions from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:54.320
their perspective. Therefore, we are primarily&nbsp;
reliant on Roman and Greek historians for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.320 --> 00:04:01.520
information on Attila and the Huns. These accounts&nbsp;
were far from neutral. For about thirty years in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:07.840
the middle of the fifth century AD, the Huns were&nbsp;
the gravest threat to the Roman Empire and Attila&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:14.320
in particular brought devastation to parts of&nbsp;
both the Balkans and Western Europe. Accordingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:20.160
the Greco-Roman historians who charted the&nbsp;
actions of Attila would have described his deeds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:26.880
in a hostile manner. The foremost contemporary&nbsp;
account of their campaigns was written by Priscus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:33.280
a Greek historian from near Constantinople, who&nbsp;
wrote a history of the Eastern Roman Empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:40.960
Priscus was a diplomat who actually visited&nbsp;
Attila’s court in 447 AD. His work has only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:48.160
survived in a fragmentary format, but it was used&nbsp;
by the sixth-century historian Jordanes in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:54.880
The Origins and Deeds of the Goths, a major work&nbsp;
on the various Germanic and Asiatic tribes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:03.680
repeatedly attacked the Roman Empire from the&nbsp;
third century AD onwards. For the Western Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:11.520
Empire, Prosper of Aquitaine’s Gallic Chronicle is&nbsp;
a key source of information as Prosper also lived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:17.120
through Attila’s attacks on the empire. There&nbsp;
are also some fragments of information in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:23.120
works like the chronicle of Hydatius of Aquae&nbsp;
Flaviae, a churchman from Gaul, while there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:29.040
are passing references to Attila and the Huns in&nbsp;
the surviving letters of Sidonius Apollinaris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:35.120
a Gallo-Roman aristocrat, who grew up against&nbsp;
the backdrop of Attila’s campaigns and remembered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:43.200
them in later life. These sources are of varying&nbsp;
quality and reliability, and are biased, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:49.920
provide enough information to at least partially&nbsp;
reconstruct the events of Attila’s life. 
&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:58.480
The one thing that these sources don’t tell us,&nbsp;
is where the Huns came from. Roman and Greek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:03.760
historians and scholars had a good idea of the&nbsp;
geography of places like Poland and the lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:09.840
north of the Black Sea stretching east along&nbsp;
the Pontic-Caspian Sea, but beyond that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:15.520
were largely unclear about the geography and&nbsp;
demography of places further afield. They had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:23.200
no real knowledge of the place the Huns came from.&nbsp;
This was the Asian Steppe, a vast flatland on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:28.400
northern side of the Himalayan Mountains, a part&nbsp;
of the world from which other tribal groups like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:34.800
the Turks and Mongols would later emerge, to&nbsp;
conquer much of Eurasia. The Huns were similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:40.320
to these later groups. Before they ever came to&nbsp;
Europe, they had possibly been raiding into China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:46.000
for centuries and are quite possibly synonymous&nbsp;
with the nomadic raiders which the Chinese knew as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:54.160
the Hsiung-Nu or Xiongnu. Later the Huns started&nbsp;
to move westwards as part of a vast migration of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.160 --> 00:07:00.240
Germanic and Asiatic people towards the Roman&nbsp;
Empire, which was occurring as people were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:06.240
pulled towards Rome’s borders by the possibility&nbsp;
of rich plunder and also pushed westwards from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:12.160
the Asian and Pontic-Caspian Steppes by climate&nbsp;
change that was impacting on their traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:18.160
pastoral subsistence. By the middle of the&nbsp;
fourth century AD, the Huns began migrating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:24.560
over the Ural Mountains and towards the River&nbsp;
Volga. As they advanced further westwards they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:31.600
pushed other Germanic and Asiatic groups onwards&nbsp;
towards Rome’s borders. Hence, the Huns were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:39.200
impacting on the Roman Empire before the Romans&nbsp;
were ever aware of who they even were. 
&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:46.800
The Roman Empire was ripe for plundering by the&nbsp;
time the Huns began to descend into what is now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:53.200
Ukraine and the Carpathian Basin at the very end&nbsp;
of the fourth century and the start of the fifth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:59.280
The empire had been divided for administrative&nbsp;
purposes into an eastern and western half in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:05.440
late third century. A hundred years later&nbsp;
the empire splintered fully into a Western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:13.120
Roman Empire and Eastern Roman Empire in 395&nbsp;
AD. Within a few years the government in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:18.640
east tried to rid themselves of the invading&nbsp;
Goths, who had largely taken over the Balkans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:25.360
by encouraging them to attack the Western&nbsp;
Roman Empire. So it was, that in 410 AD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:31.600
Alaric the Goth sacked the city of Rome. The&nbsp;
very same year, the Roman legions strategically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:38.320
withdrew from Britain, while over the next twenty&nbsp;
years groups like the Franks, Alemanni and Vandals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:44.080
conquered large parts of Gaul, Hispania and&nbsp;
even North Africa. This was the situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:51.520
which prevailed as the Huns began to move into&nbsp;
Central Europe in the early fifth century AD. 
&nbsp;

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:59.680
Although Attila is by far the best attested&nbsp;
ruler of the Huns, as Greek and Roman writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:06.240
like Priscus and Jordanes wrote extensively about&nbsp;
him, we also know a little about their leadership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:12.560
as Attila was growing up. Around the time of&nbsp;
his birth, the Huns were led by a warlord named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:19.760
Uldin. He is the first Hunnic leader mentioned by&nbsp;
name in the Greek and Roman histories of the era.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:25.440
While the Goths were raiding through the&nbsp;
Balkans and then into Italy in the mid-400s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:31.760
Uldin evidently led his men into Dacia, modern-day&nbsp;
Romania, and began conquering parts of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:38.160
province and exacting tribute from the local Roman&nbsp;
authorities in order to not raid further south or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:44.960
west. As far away as Bethlehem in the Levant,&nbsp;
St Jerome wrote of a new people he had heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:52.000
of who “had poured forth from the distant Sea of&nbsp;
Azov,” and who were “flying hither and thither on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:59.280
their swift steeds … filling the whole world with&nbsp;
bloodshed and panic.” Uldin was succeeded not long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:06.800
afterwards, by a Hun warlord named Charaton in&nbsp;
the early 410s. By then, the Huns had begun moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:13.120
into the Carpathian Basin and were emerging as the&nbsp;
chief adversary of the Eastern Roman Empire in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:20.160
Balkans. We don’t know a lot about their internal&nbsp;
politics at this early stage and it is unclear if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:28.000
either Uldin or Charaton were related to Attila&nbsp;
in any way. 
&nbsp;

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:33.520
Attila’s early life can only be guessed&nbsp;
at. The Huns were illiterate and there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:39.360
no formal schooling amongst them in the way we&nbsp;
understand it today. Instead, the education of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:45.200
a warlord’s son like Attila would have been in&nbsp;
teaching him to ride a horse and to fight from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:51.040
childhood. When he reached his teenage years, he&nbsp;
was quasi-royalty within the leadership of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:59.280
Huns. Jordanes mentions that Mundzuk, Attila’s&nbsp;
father, was the brother of Octar and Rugila.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:06.000
They were the joint-rulers of the Huns in the 420s&nbsp;
AD. It is possible that they had divided the Hun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:12.880
confederacy between them and Octar led the Western&nbsp;
Huns, while Rugila was in charge of their eastern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:19.840
lands, launching raids south against the Eastern&nbsp;
Roman Empire. Jordanes left behind a description&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:26.160
of Attila in his adult years, though there may&nbsp;
have been some artistic licence in terms of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:32.960
he described an individual whom he had never&nbsp;
met. He stated that “He had an arrogant walk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:38.640
and he rolled his eyes as he looked about. The&nbsp;
power of that haughty spirit showed through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:45.680
in every movement of his body. He certainly&nbsp;
loved war, yet he was restrained, lenient to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:52.960
those to whom he gave his protection, gracious to&nbsp;
supplicants and keen of judgement. He was short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.960 --> 00:12:00.800
with a barrel chest and a large head. He had small&nbsp;
eyes and a sparse beard flecked with grey. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:07.760
ancestry was plainly evident in his flat nose and&nbsp;
swarthy complexion.” This was the Hun who would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:16.800
become known as “the scourge of god.” 
Attila’s rise to power along with his brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:24.800
Bleda occurred around 434 AD. Their succession&nbsp;
as leaders of the Hun confederacy followed from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:31.440
the death of their uncle, Rugila. It’s not clear&nbsp;
why Attila and Bleda succeeded to the leadership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:38.960
as they did have another surviving uncle named&nbsp;
Oebarsius, who was alive in the late 440s AD.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:46.160
It seems likely that Attila and Bleda’s father,&nbsp;
Mundzuk, was already dead before Rugila died.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:52.720
The succession of Attila and Bleda as joint rulers&nbsp;
of the Huns echoed the earlier arrangement between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:58.960
Octar and Rugila and they may have had different&nbsp;
spheres of influence in the Balkans. At the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:04.640
time that they succeeded, the Huns were living&nbsp;
relatively peacefully in the Carpathian Basin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:11.120
Rugila had accepted an offer from Emperor&nbsp;
Theodosius II, the Eastern Roman Emperor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:17.840
throughout virtually the entire first half of the&nbsp;
fifth century AD, to become an uneasy ally of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:24.320
Romans, in return for not attacking their lands&nbsp;
to the south and Rugila was given the honorary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:30.880
title of a general in the Roman military. More&nbsp;
importantly, he was paid an annual tribute of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:38.400
350 pounds of gold to protect the northern border&nbsp;
of the Eastern Roman Empire. Given that the Hun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:44.960
confederacy was the primary threat to the empire’s&nbsp;
border at this time, the tribute was effectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:51.600
extortion money exacted from the imperial&nbsp;
government in return for not attacking the empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:59.120
The question for Attila and Bleda when they&nbsp;
rose to power in around 434 AD, was whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:05.760
they would continue to simply exact tributes from&nbsp;
the Romans in return for passively residing in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:13.600
Carpathian Basin, or if they would resume their&nbsp;
destructive attacks to the south. 
	
&nbsp;

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:22.320
The empire that Attila and Bleda were succeeding&nbsp;
to as joint rulers, was considerable. The Huns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:26.560
controlled a large expanse of territory,&nbsp;
stretching from around the banks of the River&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:33.280
Rhine in the west, to the Pontic-Caspian Steppe in&nbsp;
the east around modern-day Ukraine. This included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:38.320
the northern parts of the Balkans, meaning&nbsp;
that they had extended their sphere of control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:44.240
to the edge of the Western Roman Empire and were&nbsp;
occupying the northern parts of the Eastern Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:50.880
Empire. The centre of this territorial empire&nbsp;
was most likely in the Carpathian Basin, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:57.120
large flatland which lies primarily in modern-day&nbsp;
Hungary and which is surrounded by the Carpathian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:03.760
Mountains, the Balkan Mountains and the Dinaric&nbsp;
Alps. The flatlands here were an ideal place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:09.840
for the Huns to establish themselves, as they&nbsp;
were pastoralists, who rode horses into battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:15.920
lived primarily off livestock, and were used to&nbsp;
living on large flat expanses like this on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:22.720
Pontic-Caspian and Asian Steppes. Thus, although&nbsp;
we cannot be certain exactly where the centre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:29.680
of power of the Hun empire was, most studies&nbsp;
favour the idea that Attila and Bleda would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:36.240
operated from the Carpathian Basin. This was not&nbsp;
a territorial state in the modern sense or even in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:42.320
the way that the Romans exercised power. It was&nbsp;
more of a sphere of influence throughout which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:48.560
the Huns exacted tribute. Moreover, although&nbsp;
they were effectively occupying lands which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:54.400
had traditionally been part of the Roman Empire,&nbsp;
the governments of neither the Eastern or Western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.400 --> 00:16:02.640
empires were in a position to try to expel them&nbsp;
from these lands in the 430s or 440s AD. 
&nbsp;

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:10.960
The ethnic composition of the empire of the Huns&nbsp;
and of their armies was complex. In the studies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:17.280
of the so-called ‘barbarian migrations’ that were&nbsp;
written for much of the twentieth century, various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:24.000
Germanic and Asiatic groups that invaded the Roman&nbsp;
Empire between the third and fifth centuries AD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:29.920
were generally depicted as being homogenous&nbsp;
ethnic groups. Today it is recognised that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:36.720
armies that invaded the Roman Empire were much&nbsp;
more ethnically varied. While the Huns were the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:42.960
dominant group within their numbers, their armies&nbsp;
and their empire were more of a confederation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:49.600
Germanic and Asiatic groups that were allied&nbsp;
together against the Romans. These included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:54.000
the remnants of the Goths in the Balkans and&nbsp;
additional groups that had been attacking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.000 --> 00:17:00.880
Roman borders for centuries, notably the Alans&nbsp;
and Sarmatians, while people such as the Sciri&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:08.000
and Heruls were also involved. Hence, Attila&nbsp;
was not the ruler of a unitary Hunnic state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:13.280
but rather he and Bleda had ascended&nbsp;
to control a confederation of nomadic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:19.120
warrior people drawn to Rome’s borders by the&nbsp;
promise of rich plunder for those willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.120 --> 00:17:27.440
attack the declining empire. 
Attila and Bleda did not change the strategy which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:33.600
Rugila had adopted in the Balkans years earlier.&nbsp;
They renegotiated the terms of the tribute with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:40.000
Emperor Theodosius II to receive a larger amount&nbsp;
of tribute and also to increase the amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:46.160
trade between the Huns and the Romans, but the&nbsp;
broad deal remained in place for several years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:52.640
after the two brothers ascended to lead the&nbsp;
Hun confederacy. Instead, the Huns and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:58.880
other Germanic and Asiatic allies went east and&nbsp;
descended into the Caucasus to campaign against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:05.040
the northern borders of the Sassanid Persian&nbsp;
empire, a move which the Eastern Roman government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:11.920
in Constantinople, the longstanding adversary of&nbsp;
the Persians, were undoubtedly happy about. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:17.040
campaign was not as successful for the Huns&nbsp;
as earlier ones in Europe, perhaps because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:23.360
of the sheer distance from their main base of&nbsp;
operations in the Carpathian Basin. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:31.120
around 440 AD, Attila and Bleda reorientated&nbsp;
their strategy again and began attacking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:40.000
Eastern Roman Empire. In 441 AD they flattened the&nbsp;
town of Margus near the Danube River, apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:45.920
under the simple pretext that the local Christian&nbsp;
bishop had offended the sensibilities of the Huns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:52.240
who adhered to a religion with elements of&nbsp;
polytheism and animism. The years that followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:58.480
saw sporadic clashes in the northern Balkans as&nbsp;
Theodosius’ government fluctuated between trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.480 --> 00:19:04.800
to militarily challenge the Huns and simply&nbsp;
paying them off with ever larger tributes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:11.600
This changing strategy was determined in part&nbsp;
by events elsewhere as Emperor Theodosius tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:20.240
to move legions from North Africa and the Levant&nbsp;
to the Balkans. By 443 and 444 AD, the conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:29.680
was intensifying as it became apparent that&nbsp;
Theodosius intended to resist the Huns. 
&nbsp;

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:34.800
The war between the Huns and the Eastern Roman&nbsp;
Empire would reach its peak in the second half of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:44.960
the 440s AD. By then Attila was the sole ruler&nbsp;
of the Huns. Bleda died around 445 AD. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:51.040
usually stated as a fact that Attila killed his&nbsp;
own brother in order to concentrate power in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.040 --> 00:19:58.240
own hands. However, there is actually very little&nbsp;
evidence regarding Bleda’s demise. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:04.160
Jordanes stated that “when his brother Bleda,&nbsp;
who ruled over a great part of the Huns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:11.520
had been slain by his treachery, Attila united&nbsp;
all the people under his own rule.” Later medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:17.760
accounts such as the Chronicon Pictum, written&nbsp;
by Mark of Kalt in the fourteenth century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:24.960
embellished on this and stated that “Attila went&nbsp;
in the city of Sicambria in Pannonia, where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:32.160
killed Bleda, his brother, and he threw his corpse&nbsp;
into the Danube.” There really is no evidence to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:38.960
support this. The story of Attila’s murder of&nbsp;
his own brother became more elaborate over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:44.960
yet in reality, the evidence of the ancient&nbsp;
sources only implies that Attila had his brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:51.360
killed in order to claim complete power himself.&nbsp;
There isn’t much evidence, though, to say how,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.360 --> 00:20:58.000
where, when and why this happened. What is&nbsp;
beyond doubt is that Attila entered into the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:06.080
dangerous phase of his career after Bleda’s death.&nbsp;
In 447 AD the Huns burst through the borders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:12.960
of the Eastern Roman Empire and devastated the&nbsp;
provinces covering modern-day Serbia and Bulgaria.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:19.360
They won a major victory at the Battle of Utus and&nbsp;
threatened the capital, but Constantinople’s great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:27.760
walls were too formidable to penetrate. Instead,&nbsp;
Theodosius decided to once again bribe Attila,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:36.160
this time paying him an enormous tribute&nbsp;
of over 2,000 pounds of gold.   
	
	
&nbsp;

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:42.240
The organisation of peace between the Eastern&nbsp;
Roman Empire and the Huns in the aftermath of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:48.960
Battle of Utus saw Priscus, our leading source for&nbsp;
Attila’s actions in the Balkans, visit the Hunnic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:56.640
court as part of the diplomatic negotiations. This&nbsp;
was fortunate, as Priscus afterwards left behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.640 --> 00:22:01.600
a detailed description of what he saw there.&nbsp;
This clearly indicates that the court of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.600 --> 00:22:07.600
nomadic Huns was not stationary. It moved around&nbsp;
and was made from wooden structures that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:15.280
be taken apart, transported and reassembled. For&nbsp;
instance, Priscus noted that “Attila’s residence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:21.120
which was situated here, was said to be more&nbsp;
splendid than his houses in other places.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:27.360
It was made of polished boards, and surrounded&nbsp;
with wooden enclosures, designed not so much for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:34.320
protection as for appearance’ sake.” Nevertheless,&nbsp;
while they might have been moving around, and been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:40.480
a warlike people, Priscus indicates that the Huns&nbsp;
had adopted some of the luxuries of life in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:48.320
Roman Empire. For instance, he noted that Attila’s&nbsp;
second-in-command, a chieftain named Onegesius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:55.680
had a bath-house next to his house. Elsewhere&nbsp;
Priscus observed “buildings of carved boards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.680 --> 00:23:02.640
beautifully fitted together, others of straight&nbsp;
planed beams, without carving, fastened on round&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:08.240
wooden blocks which rose to a moderate height&nbsp;
from the ground,” while inside these buildings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:17.440
were furnished with couches and woollen&nbsp;
mats. This was not a primitive setting. 
&nbsp;

00:23:17.440 --> 00:23:22.800
Priscus went on to provide an account of his&nbsp;
direct encounter with the leader of the Huns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:28.240
and his observations of how the royal court&nbsp;
functioned: “He came forth from the house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:36.640
with a dignified strut, looking round on this side&nbsp;
and on that. He was accompanied by Onegesius, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:43.200
stood in front of the house; and many persons who&nbsp;
had lawsuits with one another came up and received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:51.280
his judgment. Then he returned into the house and&nbsp;
received ambassadors of barbarous peoples.” What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:56.080
is most striking about this is that the Roman&nbsp;
embassies from both the Western and Eastern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.080 --> 00:24:03.200
empires were made to wait to be called one by one&nbsp;
to come before Attila: “When the hour arrived we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:09.040
went to the palace, along with the embassy from&nbsp;
the western Romans, and stood on the threshold of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:15.520
the hall in the presence of Attila. The cupbearers&nbsp;
gave us a cup, according to the national custom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:22.800
that we might pray before we sat down. Having&nbsp;
tasted the cup, we proceeded to take our seats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:29.040
all the chairs being ranged along the walls of&nbsp;
the room on either side. Attila sat in the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:36.320
on a couch; a second couch was set behind&nbsp;
him, and from it steps led up to his bed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:42.080
which was covered with linen sheets and wrought&nbsp;
coverlets for ornament, such as Greeks and Romans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.080 --> 00:24:50.640
used to deck bridal beds.” As the evening went&nbsp;
on a feast began. This was not rustic. Fine food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:57.120
was brought in on silver platters, but Priscus&nbsp;
noted that Attila was content with more simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:04.720
fare served in a simple way: “Attila ate nothing&nbsp;
but meat on a wooden trencher. In everything else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:11.280
too, he showed himself temperate — his cup was&nbsp;
of wood, while to the guests were given goblets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:18.480
of gold and silver. His dress, too, was quite&nbsp;
simple, affecting only to be clean. The sword he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:25.440
carried at his side, the ratchets of his Scythian&nbsp;
shoes, the bridle of his horse were not adorned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:31.760
like those of the other Scythians, with gold or&nbsp;
gems or anything costly.” The only affectation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:38.800
here by the leader of the Huns was after the meal,&nbsp;
when “evening fell and torches were lit, and two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:44.480
barbarians coming forward in front of Attila&nbsp;
sang songs they had composed, celebrating his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:53.040
victories and deeds of valour in war.” 
	
When Priscus departed from the court of Attila&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:58.960
the Hun, the warlord was about to embark&nbsp;
on a new strategy. Heretofore the Huns had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.960 --> 00:26:04.480
primarily focused on attacking the Eastern&nbsp;
Roman Empire, but with peace established in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:12.640
447 AD with the government of Emperor Theodosius&nbsp;
II in Constantinople, Attila looked westwards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:20.320
The Western Roman Empire had been under the&nbsp;
rule of Emperor Valentinian III since 425 AD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.320 --> 00:26:26.400
though he had succeeded to the imperial title&nbsp;
when he was a six-year old child and only began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:32.640
to rule himself in the 440s AD. During his&nbsp;
younger years, the situation in the empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:39.120
had gone from bad to worse. Already at the time&nbsp;
of his accession, Britain had been abandoned and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:45.440
groups like the Franks, Visigoths and Vandals&nbsp;
occupied large parts of Gaul and Hispania.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.440 --> 00:26:52.560
Things became worse still in 429 AD when the&nbsp;
new ruler of the Vandals in Hispania, Gaiseric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:58.640
assembled a large fleet and led tens of thousands&nbsp;
of his followers across the Mediterranean to North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:05.840
Africa. Over the next ten years the Vandals carved&nbsp;
out their own kingdom in North Africa, stretching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:11.760
all the way from the Straits of Gibraltar to the&nbsp;
city of Carthage in modern-day Tunisia, ending the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:19.200
rule of the Western Roman Empire here. Therefore,&nbsp;
when Valentinian came of age in the 440s AD, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:25.760
western empire was broadly confined to controlling&nbsp;
Italy and parts of southern and eastern Gaul.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:25.760 --> 00:27:33.040
They still aspired to control northern and&nbsp;
western Gaul, parts of Germania and even Hispania,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:33.040 --> 00:27:39.040
but the Visigoths, Franks and Burgundians were&nbsp;
more influential in these regions and only gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:45.280
allegiance to the Romans intermittently. It&nbsp;
was against this decaying power that Attila&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:54.640
turned his forces in the late 440s AD. 
	
He would face a formidable adversary in Western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:54.640 --> 00:28:02.240
Europe, though it was not the Emperor Valentinian&nbsp;
III. This was Flavius Aetius. He was born in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.240 --> 00:28:08.640
Balkans in the late fourth century, though he was&nbsp;
descended from the old Roman aristocracy of Italy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:15.840
on his mother’s side. As a teenager he had been a&nbsp;
hostage in the entourage of Alaric the Goth as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:24.000
rampaged through Italy in the period prior to the&nbsp;
sack of Rome in 410 AD. Later Aetius spent time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:30.000
at the court of the Huns in the Balkans during&nbsp;
the era when Uldin and Charaton were the rulers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:36.080
of the Hun confederacy. Therefore, the figure&nbsp;
who would lead the defence of the Western Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:43.200
Empire against Attila’s assault in the late&nbsp;
440s and early 450s AD was very familiar with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:50.160
the Huns and their confederates in the Balkans.&nbsp;
He was also a very experienced general. Aetius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:57.520
had campaigned extensively against the Franks and&nbsp;
Visigoths in Gaul from the late 420s AD onwards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:57.520 --> 00:29:02.880
He had been comparatively successful in trying&nbsp;
to re-establish Roman control over the region&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:08.960
north from the Alps along the Rhone River towards&nbsp;
the western bank of the Rhine, the old northern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.960 --> 00:29:16.080
frontier of the empire. However, after Valentinian&nbsp;
III came of age, clashes between the emperor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:23.120
the general began. Thus, not only was the Western&nbsp;
Roman Empire being viciously attacked by Germanic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:30.320
groups like the Vandals, Visigoths and Franks that&nbsp;
were living within Gaul, Hispania and North Africa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:30.320 --> 00:29:36.240
but there was also an internal power struggle&nbsp;
between the Emperor Valentinian III and the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:44.720
powerful general of the era, Aetius. 
	
Attila’s decision to campaign westwards was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.720 --> 00:29:51.920
seemingly inspired by the sister of Emperor&nbsp;
Valentinian III, a woman named Honoria. Polygamy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.920 --> 00:29:59.040
was practised by the Huns and Attila had multiple&nbsp;
wives or concubines and many children already.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:59.040 --> 00:30:04.640
Still, he determined after establishing peace&nbsp;
terms with the Eastern Roman Empire that he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.640 --> 00:30:11.520
campaign west and marry Honoria. Remarkably,&nbsp;
she appears to have established contact with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:19.200
Attila and had requested that he come to Italy.&nbsp;
The backstory was unusual. Honoria was pregnant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:24.640
from a relationship with a court functionary&nbsp;
and her brother had placed her under a de-facto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.640 --> 00:30:30.400
house arrest when he discovered that she was&nbsp;
with child. Honoria had consequently requested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.400 --> 00:30:37.040
Attila to come to rescue her, an invitation which&nbsp;
the Hun leader interpreted as a call for him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.040 --> 00:30:43.600
come to Italy and claim her hand in marriage.&nbsp;
While he was interpreting this overture in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:43.600 --> 00:30:49.920
a self-serving manner, he also conveniently&nbsp;
decided to conclude that Honoria’s dowry or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:56.240
marriage portion would be half of the remaining&nbsp;
territories of the Western Roman Empire. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:56.240 --> 00:31:04.080
at the end of the 440s AD Attila led a large army&nbsp;
west to claim these lands and rescue his future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:10.640
bride. Ancient sources typically exaggerated&nbsp;
the size of barbarian armies and this was no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:17.360
exception. They proposed that the Huns and their&nbsp;
allies numbered upwards of half a million men who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.360 --> 00:31:23.120
streamed over the Rhine into Gaul to try to&nbsp;
conquer the remaining Roman lands there. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:23.120 --> 00:31:29.760
likelihood is that Attila led tens of thousands&nbsp;
of men into the lands of the Western Roman Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:38.240
but nowhere near the hundreds of thousands that&nbsp;
were suggested in the ancient accounts. 
&nbsp;

00:31:38.240 --> 00:31:44.320
Attila’s initial aim was to seize control of the&nbsp;
Roman lands on the west side of the Rhine and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:50.960
into Gaul around what we know today as Belgium&nbsp;
and north-eastern France. Aetius moved north to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:56.800
stop him, gathering allies amongst the Franks and&nbsp;
Visigoths who understandably believed that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:56.800 --> 00:32:03.280
had to make common cause with the Romans to avoid&nbsp;
being displaced by the Huns themselves. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:08.400
the Christian bishops of northern Gaul tried&nbsp;
to organise the immediate defence of the cities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:08.400 --> 00:32:15.840
and towns here. Some reached accommodations with&nbsp;
Attila to avoid the Huns plundering their cities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:15.840 --> 00:32:22.960
others tried to resist. It was while describing&nbsp;
this invasion that the Latin term Flagellum Dei,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:22.960 --> 00:32:29.840
‘the scourge of god’, was first used to describe&nbsp;
Attila. The Hun army that campaigned into Gaul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:29.840 --> 00:32:35.280
would have closely resembled a description&nbsp;
given in the late fourth century AD by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:42.000
Roman military commander and historian, Ammianus&nbsp;
Marcellinus. He had described them as having no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:50.000
regular battle order. Instead “they move quickly&nbsp;
and suddenly, now dispersing, now coming together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:55.200
over the ramparts and pillaging the camp&nbsp;
before their approach had become known.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:55.200 --> 00:33:00.320
He also described them using nets that they&nbsp;
threw over enemy soldiers from their horses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:00.320 --> 00:33:08.400
before closing to massacre them.
The one thing they could not do was penetrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:08.400 --> 00:33:14.880
large city walls, as they were not skilled at&nbsp;
siege warfare. Thus, the city of Orleans in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:20.720
northern Gaul was able to withstand an attack&nbsp;
by them. Not long after abandoning his attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:20.720 --> 00:33:27.200
on this city, Attila finally clashed with the army&nbsp;
Aetius had gathered at Chalons. The Battle of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:34.560
Catalaunian Fields, as it is known, was fought on&nbsp;
the 19th of September 451 AD. With perhaps a touch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:34.560 --> 00:33:42.000
of hyperbole, Jordanes later claimed that during&nbsp;
it “the fighting became hand-to-hand, fierce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:48.480
savage, confused and without the slightest&nbsp;
respite. No ancient saga has recorded such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:48.480 --> 00:33:55.680
a conflict … Our elders report that the blood from&nbsp;
the bodies of the slain turned a small brook which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:55.680 --> 00:34:02.000
flowed through the plain into a gushing river.” At&nbsp;
the end of it, Attila and his confederate forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:08.640
fell back and retreated, though this was not an&nbsp;
unequivocal victory for the Romans. Although we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:08.640 --> 00:34:15.200
can dismiss the claim made by the historian&nbsp;
Hydatius that 300,000 men died in the clash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:15.200 --> 00:34:21.360
both sides suffered heavy losses, most notably&nbsp;
the Roman’s Visigothic allies, whose king,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:21.360 --> 00:34:28.320
Theodoric, was slain in the battle. Aetius chose&nbsp;
not to follow up his victory by chasing Attila&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:35.040
towards the Rhine. In reality he was not at&nbsp;
liberty to do so. A large contingent of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:41.520
so-called ‘Roman’ army at Chalons was made up&nbsp;
of Germanic allies and with their king dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:50.240
the Visigoths entered into a civil war between&nbsp;
his sons, Theodoric and Thorismund. 
&nbsp;

00:34:50.240 --> 00:34:56.720
Attila and his forces retreated back east of the&nbsp;
Rhine into Germania and wintered there. He would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:56.720 --> 00:35:04.000
not trouble Gaul ever again. Instead he now turned&nbsp;
his attentions southwards towards Italy itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:09.680
perhaps aware that Aetius and the Emperor&nbsp;
Valentinian III were not aligned on any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:09.680 --> 00:35:15.360
real level, while also realising that the&nbsp;
stout defence he had run into in Gaul was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:15.360 --> 00:35:21.200
largely comprised of the Visigoths and Franks,&nbsp;
whereas he would encounter less resistance if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:27.440
he struck south of the Alps. He also possibly&nbsp;
wanted to emulate Alaric the Goth’s capture of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:36.800
the city of Rome back in 410 AD. Thus, in 452 AD&nbsp;
Attila led his forces south into Italy. Strangely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:36.800 --> 00:35:42.160
though, this campaign was aborted after he&nbsp;
met with Pope Leo near the Mincius River&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:42.160 --> 00:35:48.400
in northern Italy. We have an account of&nbsp;
this in Prosper of Aquitaine’s chronicle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:54.720
He states, “Attila, having once more collected&nbsp;
his forces which had been scattered in Gaul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:54.720 --> 00:36:01.200
took his way through Pannonia into Italy … to the&nbsp;
emperor and the senate, and to the Roman people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:07.360
none of all the proposed plans to oppose the&nbsp;
enemy seemed so practicable to send the legates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:07.360 --> 00:36:15.200
to the most savage king to beg for peace. Our most&nbsp;
blessed Pope Leo – trusting to the help of God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.200 --> 00:36:21.600
who never fails the righteous in their trials&nbsp;
– undertook the task … the outcome was what his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:21.600 --> 00:36:27.760
faith had foreseen, for when the king had received&nbsp;
the embassy, he was so impressed by the presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:34.560
of the high priest that he ordered his army to&nbsp;
give up warfare, and after he had promised peace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:34.560 --> 00:36:43.520
he departed beyond the Danube.” Hence, in 451&nbsp;
AD Attila was rebuffed in his invasion of Gaul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:52.800
while a year later he was convinced by Pope Leo to&nbsp;
call off his invasion of Italy. 
	
&nbsp;

00:36:52.800 --> 00:36:58.960
Undoubtedly the descriptions of Attila and Leo’s&nbsp;
meeting were written with a Christian bias. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:58.960 --> 00:37:06.160
version even has St Peter and St Paul appearing to&nbsp;
help Leo convince Attila to abandon his campaign.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:06.160 --> 00:37:12.800
The likelihood is that Attila had invaded northern&nbsp;
Italy to try to restore his reputation and pride&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:18.240
after the defeat in Gaul the previous autumn, and&nbsp;
after plundering the towns of northern Italy he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:23.360
abandoned the campaign under the pretext&nbsp;
of having reached an agreement with Leo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:23.360 --> 00:37:28.880
He might have also realised after travelling&nbsp;
through Germania, northern Gaul and northern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:28.880 --> 00:37:35.120
Italy that the Huns were better off attacking the&nbsp;
much wealthier Eastern Roman Empire after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:35.120 --> 00:37:40.320
rather than the poor husk of what remained&nbsp;
of the Western Roman Empire. Hence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.320 --> 00:37:46.000
after his meeting with Leo, Attila led his&nbsp;
men back to the Carpathian Basin and began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:52.720
preparing for a fresh campaign in the Balkans.&nbsp;
A new emperor named Marcian had ascended to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:58.160
rule over the Eastern Roman Empire after the&nbsp;
nearly half-century long rule of Theodosius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:05.200
II. He had even launched a campaign into&nbsp;
the Hun territories in 452 AD while Attila&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:05.200 --> 00:38:11.120
was distracted in Western Europe. Therefore,&nbsp;
Attila was set on shoring up his position in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:11.120 --> 00:38:18.400
Balkans as he moved back eastwards. The intended&nbsp;
campaign would never take place though. 
&nbsp;

00:38:20.800 --> 00:38:25.920
Attila was still only in his late forties&nbsp;
at this time, perhaps nearing his fiftieth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:31.280
year. He could have continued to plague the&nbsp;
Romans in both the west and east for many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:31.280 --> 00:38:38.240
years more had his life not been cut short under&nbsp;
suspicious circumstances. Whether he was poisoned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.240 --> 00:38:44.400
or died by some alternative form of treachery&nbsp;
remains a point of debate amongst historians.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:44.400 --> 00:38:50.640
The most detailed account of his death comes&nbsp;
from Jordanes, who quotes Priscus. Jordanes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:50.640 --> 00:38:57.120
tells us that shortly before he died, Attila&nbsp;
married a certain Ildico, possibly the daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:57.120 --> 00:39:04.160
of one of his Germanic allies based on her name.&nbsp;
She was one of several wives. The wedding feast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:04.160 --> 00:39:11.280
was the occasion of his death. Jordanes states&nbsp;
that “He had given himself up to excessive joy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:18.720
at his wedding, and as he lay on his back, heavy&nbsp;
with wine and sleep, a rush of superfluous blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:18.720 --> 00:39:24.240
which would ordinarily have flowed from his&nbsp;
nose, streamed in deadly course down his throat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:24.240 --> 00:39:31.440
and killed him, since it was hindered in the usual&nbsp;
passages. Thus, did drunkenness put a disgraceful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:31.440 --> 00:39:38.640
end to a king renowned in war. On the following&nbsp;
day, when a great part of the morning was spent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:38.640 --> 00:39:45.680
the royal attendants suspected some ill and,&nbsp;
after a great uproar, broke in the doors. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:51.600
they found the death of Attila accomplished&nbsp;
by an effusion of blood, without any wound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:51.600 --> 00:39:59.280
and Ildico with downcast face weeping beneath&nbsp;
her veil. Then, as is the custom of that race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:59.280 --> 00:40:05.040
they plucked out the hair of their heads and&nbsp;
made their faces hideous with deep wounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:05.040 --> 00:40:11.760
that the renowned warrior might be mourned, not&nbsp;
by effeminate wailings and tears, but by the blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:11.760 --> 00:40:18.080
of men.” Perhaps it was a haemorrhage of some&nbsp;
kind brought on by excessive drinking, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.080 --> 00:40:24.240
some historians believe based on this account that&nbsp;
Attila was poisoned in the course of the wedding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:24.240 --> 00:40:33.280
feast and died during the night.  
News of Attila’s death went out across Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:33.280 --> 00:40:39.200
Indeed, Jordanes claims that the event was so&nbsp;
momentous that Emperor Marcian had a vision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:39.200 --> 00:40:45.920
from god alerting him to the passing of the&nbsp;
Romans’ greatest nemesis, “for so terrible was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:45.920 --> 00:40:52.480
Attila thought to be to great empires that the&nbsp;
gods announced his death to rulers as a special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:52.480 --> 00:40:59.200
boon.” In the aftermath of his suspicious death,&nbsp;
several of Attila’s sons attempted to take his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:59.200 --> 00:41:06.880
place. These were Ellac, Ernak and Dengizich.&nbsp;
A division of power between them would not have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:06.880 --> 00:41:13.120
been out of the ordinary, as Attila and Bleda had&nbsp;
been joint rulers of the confederacy for a decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:13.120 --> 00:41:19.840
before Bleda’s death. However, Attila’s sons&nbsp;
immediately tried to claim power for themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:19.840 --> 00:41:26.800
and tore the Hun confederacy apart in the process.&nbsp;
In the vacuum that was created, one of Attila’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:26.800 --> 00:41:32.320
former commanders, a man named Ardaric, who was&nbsp;
a member of the Gepids, a Germanic tribe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.320 --> 00:41:37.680
formed part of the Hun confederacy, seized&nbsp;
power after defeating Attila’s sons at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:37.680 --> 00:41:44.960
Battle of Nedao in 454 AD. He established&nbsp;
a new confederacy under Gepid leadership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:44.960 --> 00:41:52.000
one which was ultimately more enduring that that&nbsp;
of the Huns. For instance, a Gepid ruler named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:52.000 --> 00:41:58.000
Mundo or Mundus was still an important individual&nbsp;
amongst the Germanic tribes in the Carpathian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:58.000 --> 00:42:04.480
Basin three-quarters of a century later. He&nbsp;
is believed to have been Ardaric’s grandson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:04.480 --> 00:42:09.600
These developments once again suggest that&nbsp;
the empire of Attila the Hun was really just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:09.600 --> 00:42:15.920
a confederacy of Germanic and Asiatic tribes&nbsp;
based out of the Carpathian Basin in which the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:15.920 --> 00:42:21.520
Huns were briefly the pre-eminent group between&nbsp;
the early fifth century and the time of Attila’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.520 --> 00:42:28.240
death. After his passing and the overthrow of&nbsp;
his sons, the Huns faded away as a power and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:28.240 --> 00:42:37.840
were replaced by newcomers like the Gepids. 
As transient as the Hun confederacy had been,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:37.840 --> 00:42:44.480
its timing was significant. The Eastern Roman&nbsp;
Empire recovered in the medium term and entered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.480 --> 00:42:49.920
back into a period of rejuvenation in the&nbsp;
sixth century. During the long reign of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:49.920 --> 00:42:58.240
Emperor Justinian between 527 AD and 565 AD, the&nbsp;
Eastern Empire even managed to re-conquer parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:58.240 --> 00:43:05.040
of the Western Mediterranean around modern-day&nbsp;
Tunisia, Catalonia, Sicily and southern Italy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:05.040 --> 00:43:11.120
although the rise of Islam and the Arab Conquests&nbsp;
of the seventh century, combined with new waves of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:11.120 --> 00:43:17.440
Slavic invaders from Asia into the Balkans&nbsp;
reduced the Eastern Empire to a rump state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:17.440 --> 00:43:24.640
hundreds of years later. The impact of the Huns on&nbsp;
the Western Roman Empire was more dramatic. Only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:24.640 --> 00:43:32.000
23 years after the death of Attila the Hun, the&nbsp;
last Western Roman Emperor, Romulus Augustulus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:32.000 --> 00:43:39.680
was deposed by a Germanic warlord named Odoacer.&nbsp;
This event in 476 AD is seen as marking the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:39.680 --> 00:43:45.120
of the Western Roman Empire. In medieval&nbsp;
times the view consequently emerged that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:45.120 --> 00:43:50.960
Attila’s campaigns into Italy and other parts of&nbsp;
Western Europe had fatally weakened the Western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:50.960 --> 00:43:58.240
Empire and paved the way for its termination 23&nbsp;
years after his death. This interpretation had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:58.240 --> 00:44:04.320
extra credence given that only two years after&nbsp;
Attila’s death, the Vandals had crossed to Italy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:04.320 --> 00:44:10.720
from North Africa and became the second Germanic&nbsp;
group in less than half a century to sack Rome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:10.720 --> 00:44:20.560
when they did so in 455 AD. 
It was owing to the belief that Attila had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:20.560 --> 00:44:25.920
fatally weakened the Western Roman Empire and&nbsp;
paved the way for the Vandal assault and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:31.600
deposition of Romulus Augustulus that Attila&nbsp;
later became known as the major figure in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:31.600 --> 00:44:38.000
fall of the Western Roman Empire. Elaborate&nbsp;
myths concerning Attila developed as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:44.640
One held that he had possessed the sword of Mars,&nbsp;
the Roman god of war. Elements of his life story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:44.640 --> 00:44:50.240
were even transmitted north to Scandinavia and&nbsp;
were incorporated into Norse myths that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:50.240 --> 00:44:57.440
written down in the High Middle Ages 700 to 800&nbsp;
years after Attila’s own time. He was central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:57.440 --> 00:45:03.520
to the Middle High German poem, ‘The Song of the&nbsp;
Nibelungs’, while Ludwig von Beethoven considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:03.520 --> 00:45:08.880
writing an opera about Attila and he featured&nbsp;
in numerous paintings produced in the nineteenth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:08.880 --> 00:45:15.200
century. There is no obvious reason why Attila&nbsp;
acquired such a position of infamy when he never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:15.200 --> 00:45:21.520
sacked Rome like Alaric the Goth had, nor did&nbsp;
he conquer entire provinces the way the Vandals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:21.520 --> 00:45:29.760
had in North Africa. Nevertheless, the myth of&nbsp;
Attila grew and grew over the centuries. 
&nbsp;

00:45:31.760 --> 00:45:38.160
Attila the Hun’s name has become a byword for&nbsp;
the violent invasion of the Roman Empire which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:38.160 --> 00:45:44.080
brought the western half of the empire to an&nbsp;
end in the fifth century AD. He holds a place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:44.080 --> 00:45:49.440
in the western imagination similar to that&nbsp;
of Genghis Khan as an Asiatic warlord whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:55.840
forces were virtually invincible. The reality&nbsp;
is actually quite different. Almost nothing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:55.840 --> 00:46:01.680
known about his background and early life, though&nbsp;
he clearly came from a family that was involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:01.680 --> 00:46:08.640
in the leadership of the Hun confederacy in the&nbsp;
early fifth century. In the mid-430s AD, he and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:08.640 --> 00:46:15.040
his brother Bleda became the new leaders, though&nbsp;
Attila appears to have eventually orchestrated the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:15.040 --> 00:46:22.560
murder of his brother and became sole ruler of the&nbsp;
confederacy in the mid-440s AD. This being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:22.560 --> 00:46:28.000
it is important to remember that the Huns were not&nbsp;
a homogenous group that threatened the Western and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:34.640
Eastern Roman Empire for decades. They were the&nbsp;
foremost group amongst a confederacy of Germanic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:34.640 --> 00:46:41.600
and Asiatic tribes based out of the Carpathian&nbsp;
Basin. There is no doubt that Attila plagued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:41.600 --> 00:46:48.640
both the Eastern and Western halves of the empire,&nbsp;
but this needs to be viewed in context. While the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:48.640 --> 00:46:55.120
Eastern Roman Empire still had some vitality to it&nbsp;
and would rebound strongly from its difficulties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:55.120 --> 00:47:01.520
in the sixth century, the Western Roman Empire&nbsp;
was in its death throes already by the time Attila&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:01.520 --> 00:47:08.640
became leader of the Hun confederacy. Rome had&nbsp;
been sacked in 410 AD and groups like the Angles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:08.640 --> 00:47:15.760
Saxons, Franks, Visigoths and Vandals had&nbsp;
already overrun most of it. Attila clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:15.760 --> 00:47:21.040
weakened it further and left an impression&nbsp;
on contemporaries and medieval chroniclers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:21.040 --> 00:47:27.360
but he was just one of several prominent Germanic&nbsp;
and Asiatic leaders who brought the Roman Empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:27.360 --> 00:47:34.000
to the verge of ruin in the fourth and fifth&nbsp;
centuries. It is arguable that Alaric the Goth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:40.640
the warlord who sacked Rome in 410 AD, or&nbsp;
Gaiseric, the Vandal king who conquered North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:40.640 --> 00:47:48.400
Africa and then sacked Rome for a second time in&nbsp;
455 AD, were far more consequential in the fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:48.400 --> 00:47:55.440
of the Western Roman Empire. Nonetheless, it was&nbsp;
Attila who has become the paragon of the barbarian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:55.440 --> 00:48:05.600
warlord who destroyed the Western Roman Empire and&nbsp;
plunged Western Europe into the Dark Ages. 
&nbsp;

00:48:05.600 --> 00:48:11.760
What do you think of Attila the Hun? Was he really&nbsp;
the most powerful adversary the Romans faced in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:11.760 --> 00:48:19.680
late antiquity, or has his status as the so-called&nbsp;
‘scourge of god’ been strangely overstated?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:19.680 --> 00:48:43.200
Please let us know in the comment section, and in&nbsp;
the meantime, thank you very much for watching.

